#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105386 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "9fa21896-8127-5ec5-aff0-7f811403db8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12442" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(3,-1,7)$ and parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105387 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points"}] :question/id #uuid "d1adb6fb-cd44-5a12-b892-6a6d2c99929d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12443" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,7⟩-⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105388 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"}] :question/id #uuid "55ee3b10-087f-521e-ba37-12541e199408" :question/math-academy-id "e-12444" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,2,0)$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}= \\langle -4,1,1 \\rangle.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $PA=6\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩)-⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=6\\sqrt{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PA & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{18{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\pm 3t\\sqrt{2} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨1,2,0⟩+2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨-7,4,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨1,2,0⟩-2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨9,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105389 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/id #uuid "69c3fc44-8b4d-521e-b207-0ea01d5921e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12445" :question/problem "Using $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j},\\mathbf{k}$ notation, find a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(-4,1,2)$ and $Q(-2,5,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+5\\,j-k)-(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{r} = (-4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}) + t (2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\, \\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\,i+(4t+1)\\,j+(2-3t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb34a150-e343-5f14-b288-386875381a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,2,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(-5,3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311113} {:answer/id #uuid "c8456756-54ca-592c-a289-99edc3aa3d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,-5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311114} {:answer/id #uuid "a406d08d-e6bb-5c61-8aad-1593b1da44c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,1,-5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311115} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9c966a-5aed-50c4-b30e-c9cc186fe8a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311116} {:answer/id #uuid "641b04fa-ffe5-5154-b59c-31c024713831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c9c966a-5aed-50c4-b30e-c9cc186fe8a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311116} :answer-field/id #uuid "34a23037-a100-5f54-85a7-3c0b9303e5a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cbeb3212-31dc-5cc0-971a-bf4846f229e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126992" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,-2,3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-2,1,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩)-⟨3,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\,t,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=3,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =3 \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =3 \\\\ |⟨-2t,\\,t,\\,2t⟩| & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\pm 3t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,-2,3⟩+⟨-2,1,2⟩=⟨1,-1,5⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,-2,3⟩-⟨-2,1,2⟩=⟨5,-3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-3,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9a26130-b9ff-57cc-bb11-c79826a6b802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,-7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311120} {:answer/id #uuid "52dede04-bba1-5c39-a410-2592c55d3b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311121} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e62b70-6be7-5357-9bca-77c48e37e961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,-1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311122} {:answer/id #uuid "f3804c30-94da-5f7a-b59b-deffb0ce5f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311123} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb5cfbc-22c3-528a-a30a-5c064ab2aa50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,-7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52dede04-bba1-5c39-a410-2592c55d3b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311121} :answer-field/id #uuid "767e4e2d-0457-5fea-a1d0-540a9ba8e186" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "59a52ddc-339d-53d5-a5c4-8b8787ceb738" :question/math-academy-id "q-126995" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(2,3,6)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-3,-2,1⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩)-⟨2,3,6⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-3,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-3t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨2,3,6⟩+⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨-1,1,7⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨5,5,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85617a24-64b7-5da1-9338-83bce6bbcd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311134} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0147ca-bcd1-583b-ad66-678a590c7e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311135} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3e2f1d-ced2-5713-977d-d3119e1b264e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311136} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc57ed6-6e6e-51ee-95b3-3a3319fe89a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311137} {:answer/id #uuid "c57adfc5-5ef8-51f2-8585-960b427e6ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85617a24-64b7-5da1-9338-83bce6bbcd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311134} :answer-field/id #uuid "40261d74-6aea-550b-97b3-972cdc7008f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12c5ba63-0750-5dc9-b617-46b3345e0ebe" :question/math-academy-id "q-162169" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(5,0,4)$ and $Q(-3,1,5).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ a \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+1+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ddaaa1-51e9-5256-8ec2-61b60dbea226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,3,3⟩+⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311176} {:answer/id #uuid "b5dde3bd-6987-5975-9067-1e687e3b1c3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,-3,3⟩+t⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},3,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311177} {:answer/id #uuid "bea8ca7c-c0d4-50e3-85ae-c87e5205c673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-5,3,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}⟩+t⟨3,3,-3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311178} {:answer/id #uuid "653bf4c9-c0a3-559c-9e86-26f8847fa2f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩+t⟨-3,3,3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311179} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca224dd-5998-560d-aa59-8614d17185e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,3,3⟩+t⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ca224dd-5998-560d-aa59-8614d17185e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,3,3⟩+t⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311180} :answer-field/id #uuid "709b2df5-e690-57c3-b850-b19b6bbfc11a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "010422a7-4def-50ef-8089-4e9bf073b56f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35965" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q(-3,3,3)$ and parallel to $v=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=q+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $Q$ is $q=⟨-3,3,3⟩.$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨-3,3,3⟩+t⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03abd29-c10c-59d4-890c-d33705a6c216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103788} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2047da-f150-5c9b-bb00-2601831b89e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103789} {:answer/id #uuid "1398080d-65f0-590a-8f15-27fdd911ec90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + t\\